sections.wikitext:
[[File:Murray The K (face).jpg|left|thumb|Cavaliere credits the [[disc jockey]] [[Murray the K]] with pressuring [[Atlantic Records]] into releasing "Groovin{{'"}}{{Sfn|Cornish|Miller|2019|p=154}}]]
After recording had been completed, [[Jerry Wexler]], the head of Atlantic Records, was initially skeptical about releasing "Groovin{{'"}} as a single due to its vastly different musical style compared to the band's previous singles.{{Sfn|Myers|2016|p=90}} The song was thus nearly shelved, had it not been for a chance meeting with disc jockey [[Murray the K]]. According to Cavaliere, Murray the K had been present during the recording session of "Groovin{{'"}}. While listening to the playback of the song, he "went nuts" and stated that it was a "certain smash".{{Sfn|Myers|2013}} 

Wexler persisted in insisting that the Young Rascals at least added a conventional drum part to the record, to which Cavaliere insisted that it was a homage to the "whole world of [[Latin Americans|Latin people]] out there who love to dance".{{Sfn|Myers|2013}} The final straw in releasing "Groovin{{'"}} came from Murray the K, who spoke to Wexler only a few days after it was recorded.{{Sfn|Puterbaugh|1992|p=37}}{{Sfn|Myers|2013}}{{Sfn|Myers|2016|p=90}} Murray the K convinced Wexler by stating that he would play it on his radio show, ensuring it would become a number one hit.{{Sfn|Cornish|Miller|2019|p=154}}

"Groovin{{'"}} was released in the US on April 10, 1967, backed by another song written by Cavaliere and Brigati, "Sueño".{{Sfn|Cornish|Miller|2019|p=154}}{{Sfn|Puterbaugh|1992|p=3}}{{Refn|Catalogue number Atlantic 45-2401.{{sfn|Puterbaugh|1992|p=3}}|group=nb}} On the ''Billboard'' Hot 100, the single entered on April 22 at number 79.{{Sfn|Whitburn|1990|pp=523–524}} It peaked at number one a month later on May 20, becoming the Young Rascals' second number one single and top-ten entry on the ''Billboard'' Hot 100.{{Sfn|Whitburn|1990|pp=523–524}} It initially spent two consecutive weeks at number one before being dislodged by [[Aretha Franklin]]'s [[Cover version|cover]] of "[[Respect (song)|Respect]]" on June 2.{{Sfn|Whitburn|1990|p=201}} "Respect" spent two weeks at number one before "Groovin{{'"}} once again reached number one on June 17, staying there for two weeks.{{Sfn|Whitburn|1990|pp=523–524}} "Groovin{{'"}} dropped off the ''Billboard'' Hot 100 chart completely from a position of 38 on July 17.{{Sfn|Whitburn|1990|pp=523–524}} At the time, "Groovin{{'"}} beat the [[List of Billboard Hot 100 chart achievements and milestones|''Billboard'' milestone]] of dropping off the charts entirely the fastest after reaching number one.{{Sfn|Whitburn|1990|p=14}} Elsewhere in North America, "Groovin{{'"}} also reached number three on the ''Billboard'' [[Hot R&B/Hip-Hop Songs|Hot Rhythm & Blues Singles]] chart,{{Sfn|Whitburn|2017|p=482}} along with topping the [[Cashbox (magazine)|''Cash Box'']] top 100 singles and R&B chart.<ref name=":0">{{Cite journal |date=May 20, 1967 |title=Cash Box TOP 100 |url=https://worldradiohistory.com/Archive-All-Music/Cash-Box/60s/1967/CB-1967-05-20.pdf |journal=[[Cashbox (magazine)|Cash Box]] |page=4 |via=WorldRadioHistory}}</ref><ref name=":1">{{Cite journal |date=May 13, 1967 |title=TOP 50 IN R&B LOCATIONS |url=https://worldradiohistory.com/Archive-All-Music/Cash-Box/60s/1967/CB-1967-05-13.pdf |journal=[[Cashbox (magazine)|Cash Box]] |page=40 |via=WorldRadioHistory}}</ref> In Canada, it reached number one on both the ''[[RPM (magazine)|RPM]]'' Top 100 and R&B chart.<ref name=":2">{{Cite journal |date=June 3, 1967 |title=RPM 100 |url=https://worldradiohistory.com/CANADA/RPM/60s/1967/RPM-1967-06-03.pdf |journal=[[RPM (magazine)|RPM]] |page=5 |via=WorldRadioHistory}}</ref><ref name=":3">{{Cite journal |date=June 3, 1967 |title=RPM 25 R&B List |url=https://worldradiohistory.com/CANADA/RPM/60s/1967/RPM-1967-06-03.pdf |journal=[[RPM (magazine)|RPM]] |page=4 |via=WorldRadioHistory}}</ref>

In the UK, "Groovin{{'"}} was released on May 5, 1967.<ref>{{Cite journal |date=April 29, 1967 |title=Double A side release for Elvis Presley |url=https://worldradiohistory.com/UK/Record-Mirror/60s/67/Record-Mirror-1967-04-29.pdf |journal=[[Record Mirror]] |page=4 |via=WorldRadioHistory}}</ref> It entered the ''[[Record Retailer]]'' chart on May 31 at a position of 48, before peaking at number eight on July 4.{{Sfn|Brown|2000|p=231}} It dropped off the charts on August 22 at a position of 48, having spent 13 weeks in total.{{Sfn|Brown|2000|p=231}} "Groovin{{'"}} became the Young Rascals' only top-20 single in the UK.{{Sfn|Brown|2000|p=231}}{{Refn|The Young Rascals only other charting single in the UK was "[[A Girl Like You (The Young Rascals song)|A Girl Like You]]", which reached number 37.{{sfn|Brown|2000|p=231}}|group=nb}} Elsewhere in the world, "Groovin{{'"}} became a top-twenty hit in [[Oceania]], reaching number three in Australia and number 13 in New Zealand.<ref name=":4">{{Cite web |title=Listener |url=https://www.flavourofnz.co.nz/index.php?qpageID=search%20listener#n_view_location |access-date=2022-08-30 |publisher=[[New Zealand Listener]] |archive-date=September 22, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220922090847/https://www.flavourofnz.co.nz/index.php?qpageID=search%20listener#n_view_location |url-status=dead }}</ref> In Africa, "Groovin{{'"}} reached the top-ten in both [[South Africa]] and [[Rhodesia]], peaking at number ten and three respectively.<ref>{{cite web |title=SA Charts 1965 – March 1989 |url-status=dead| archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190325202631/http://rock.co.za/files/springbok_top_20_(Y).html |archive-date=March 25, 2019| url=http://rock.co.za/files/springbok_top_20_(Y).html |access-date=August 20, 2022 |website=Rock.co.za}}</ref>{{Sfn|Kimberley|2000|p=50}} Despite "Groovin{{'"}} finding massive commercial success on multiple continents, its commercial performance was lackluster in [[Continental Europe]], where it failed to enter the top-ten on any national chart.{{Sfn|Cornish|Miller|2019|p=165}} It did reach the top-twenty in the [[Netherlands]] and [[West Germany]], peaking at number 16 and 20 respectively.<ref>{{Cite web |title=The Young Rascals - Groovin |url=https://www.top40.nl/the-young-rascals/the-young-rascals-groovin-2921 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230603141955/https://www.top40.nl/the-young-rascals/the-young-rascals-groovin-2921 |archive-date=June 3, 2023 |access-date=August 20, 2023 |publisher=[[Dutch Top 40]]}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=The Young Rascals - Groovin' |url=https://www.offiziellecharts.de/titel-details-28948 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161201111742/https://www.offiziellecharts.de/titel-details-28948 |archive-date=December 1, 2016 |access-date=August 20, 2023 |publisher=[[GfK Entertainment charts]]}}</ref>

Due to the massive popularity of "Groovin{{'"}}, the single was chosen to be the title track of the band's [[Groovin' (The Young Rascals album)| third studio album]], which was released by Atlantic Records on July 31, 1967.{{Sfn|Cornish|Miller|2019|p=165}}{{Sfn|Puterbaugh|1992|p=5}} On mono copies of the album, the single version of "Groovin{{'"}} is included, while stereo copies include the alternate harmonica performance by Cornish.{{Sfn|Myers|2013}}{{Sfn|Puterbaugh|1992|pp=3, 6}} Fueled primarily by the success of "Groovin{{'"}}, "[[A Girl Like You (The Young Rascals song)|A Girl Like You]]" and "[[How Can I Be Sure?]]" (both 1967), ''Groovin''' reached number five on the [[Billboard 200|''Billboard'' 200]], becoming their highest charting studio album.{{Sfn|Whitburn|2007|p=484}} "Groovin{{'"}} has also appeared on most of the Young Rascals' [[Compilation album|compilation albums]], the first of which was ''[[Time Peace: The Rascals' Greatest Hits]]'' that was released by Atlantic a year after the single on June 24, 1968.{{Sfn|Cornish|Miller|2019|p=165}}{{Sfn|Puterbaugh|1992|p=5}} The album reached number one on the ''Billboard'' 200.{{Sfn|Whitburn|2007|p=484}}

