[[Image:The Rascals - PNC 2013 - Groovin 2.jpg|thumb|238px|The Rascals performing "Groovin{{'"}} during their 2013 ''Once Upon a Dream'' show.]]Retrospectively, "Groovin{{'"}} has also received praise from critics, with many noting the song to be an experimental endeavour for the Young Rascals who would continue releasing increasingly [[Psychedelic music|psychedelic]] and experimental music.{{Sfn|Cornish|Miller|2019|p=154}} [[AllMusic]]'s Lindsay Planer stated that though the song retained the "same passion that drove" their previous singles, "Groovin{{'"}} was an "easy going ballad" that evoked a "pastoral setting with the novel inclusion of bird call sound effects".<ref name=":9">{{Cite web |last=Planer |first=Lindsay |title=Groovin - Song review by Lindsay Planer |url=https://www.allmusic.com/song/groovin-mt0013721076 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230821215128/https://www.allmusic.com/song/groovin-mt0013721076 |archive-date=August 21, 2023 |access-date=August 21, 2023 |website=[[AllMusic]]}}</ref> 

According to reporter Parke Puterbaugh, "Groovin{{'"}} marked the gap between the Young Rascals earlier rhythm & blues-based music to more "mellower, sunnier and romantic" music.{{Sfn|Puterbaugh|1992|p=37}} Journalist [[Marc Myers]] considers "Groovin{{'"}} to be a nod to the Young Rascals Latin American fans, owing to the strong influence from baião music the single has.{{Sfn|Myers|2016|p=90}} Music critic [[Richie Unterberger]] states that the band's fans most likely were unprepared for how the Rascals changed up their music, with the song's "breezy feel" that he called as "carefree as a summer walk".{{Sfn|Unterberger|2007b|p=1}}

Despite being released during the spring of 1967, "Groovin{{'"}} peaked on the ''Billboard'' charts in May and June which Planer considered to be a "perfect promotion to the upcoming [[Summer of Love]]" phenomenon that would sweep across America during that summer.<ref name=":9" /> Puterbaugh states that it was a perfect record for "reflecting off of [[Fire escape|fire escapes]] and terraces" as "it was #1 as the day dawned upon the new rock era".{{Sfn|Puterbaugh|1992|p=20}} Cornish states that the song gave the Young Rascals some slight newfound respect amongst underground [[hippie]] movements due to the more experimental, relaxed nature of it.{{Sfn|Cornish|Miller|2019|p=154}} "Groovin{{'"}} also prompted the Young Rascals to change up their appearance, switching up their clothing for "Nehru shirts, beards and beads", along with prompting them to drop the 'Young'-prefix from their named.{{Sfn|Puterbaugh|1992|pp=20–21}}{{Refn|The final release on which the band was labelled 'the Young Rascals' was "[[It's Wonderful]]", released in November 1967.{{sfn|Cornish|Miller|2019|p=153}}|group=nb}}

The Young Rascals themselves were scheduled to appear on ''[[The Ed Sullivan Show]]'' twice owing to the success of "Groovin{{'"}}, performing it live on June 4 and September 10, 1967 respectively.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Sexton |first=Paul |date=August 8, 2022 |title=Chill Out With The Young Rascals, 'Groovin'<nowiki/>' On 'The Ed Sullivan Show' |work=uDiscoverMusic |url=https://www.udiscovermusic.com/news/young-rascals-groovin-ed-sullivan-show/ |url-status=live |access-date=August 21, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230129162837/https://www.udiscovermusic.com/news/young-rascals-groovin-ed-sullivan-show/ |archive-date=January 29, 2023}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Sexton |first=Paul |date=July 17, 2023 |title='Ed Sullivan Show' Archive Shares Young Rascals' Summer Of Love Gem |work=uDiscoverMusic |url=https://www.udiscovermusic.com/news/ed-sullivan-show-young-rascals-summer-of-love/ |url-status=live |access-date=August 21, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230719155132/https://www.udiscovermusic.com/news/ed-sullivan-show-young-rascals-summer-of-love/ |archive-date=July 19, 2023}}</ref> The song was an [[Recording Industry Association of America|RIAA]]-certified [[RIAA certification|gold record]] on June 13, 1967 for 500,000 copies sold.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Gold & Platinum |url=https://www.riaa.com/gold-platinum/?tab_active=default-award&ar=YOUNG+RASCALS |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230821222318/https://www.riaa.com/gold-platinum/?tab_active=default-award&ar=YOUNG+RASCALS |archive-date=August 21, 2023 |access-date=August 22, 2023 |publisher=[[Recording Industry Association of America]]}}</ref> "Groovin{{'"}} is one of [[the Rock and Roll Hall of Fame's 500 Songs that Shaped Rock and Roll]],<ref>{{cite web |title=FAQ |url=http://www.rockhall.com/site/faq/ |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150322185411/http://rockhall.com/site/faq/ |archive-date=March 22, 2015 |access-date=March 24, 2015 |website=The Rock and Roll Hall of Fame and Museum}}</ref> and is also the recipient of a [[List of Grammy Hall of Fame Award recipients E-I|Grammy Hall of Fame Award]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Grammy Hall Of Fame |url=https://www.grammy.com/awards/hall-of-fame |url-status=usurped |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150626200735/https://www.grammy.org/recording-academy/awards/hall-of-fame |archive-date=June 26, 2015 |access-date=August 22, 2023 |publisher=[[The Recording Academy]]}}</ref>

